  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "There are $g>0$ green marbles and $b>0$ blue marbles in a bag. Two marbles are drawn from the bag without replacement. Let $G$ be the event that the first marble drawn is green, and let $B$ be the event that the second marble drawn is blue.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q8a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $\\Pr(G)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{g}{g+b}$",
          "worked": "$$\\Pr(G)=\\frac{g}{g+b}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q8b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $\\Pr(B)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{b}{g+b}$\n\nAdd the green–blue and blue–blue cases.",
          "worked": "The first marble is either green or blue. Adding these disjoint cases,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(B)&=\\frac{g}{g+b}\\times\\frac{b}{g+b-1}+\\frac{b}{g+b}\\times\\frac{b-1}{g+b-1}\\\\\\Pr(B)&=\\frac{gb+b(b-1)}{(g+b)(g+b-1)}\\\\\\Pr(B)&=\\frac{b(g+b-1)}{(g+b)(g+b-1)}\\\\\\Pr(B)&=\\frac{b}{g+b}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q8c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find $\\Pr(G\\cap B)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{gb}{(g+b)(g+b-1)}$",
          "worked": "After drawing a green marble, $b$ blue marbles remain among $g+b-1$ marbles.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(G\\cap B)&=\\frac{g}{g+b}\\times\\frac{b}{g+b-1}\\\\\\Pr(G\\cap B)&=\\frac{gb}{(g+b)(g+b-1)}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q8d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Are $G$ and $B$ independent?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No.\n\n$\\Pr(G)\\Pr(B)=\\frac{gb}{(g+b)^2}\\ne\\Pr(G\\cap B)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\Pr(G)\\Pr(B)=\\frac{g}{g+b}\\times\\frac{b}{g+b}=\\frac{gb}{(g+b)^2}.$$\nBut\n$$\\Pr(G\\cap B)=\\frac{gb}{(g+b)(g+b-1)}.$$\nSince $g,b>0$, the numerators are positive and the denominators differ. Therefore, $G$ and $B$ are not independent.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
